---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo';
const title = 'ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Compare Shopify recovery and wholesale follow-up in ActiveCampaign and Klaviyo: event coverage, CRM scope, migration and a matched cost worksheet.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which is better for a Shopify store that sells mostly direct to consumer?",
    "answer": "Start by evaluating Klaviyo for order-driven retention, but do not assume a performance advantage. Compare its checkout and order events with the events your store actually produces. ActiveCampaign also has a documented Shopify abandoned-cart integration; an existing working setup is a reason to evaluate migration costs before replacing it."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do the two tools compare on price at 1,000 contacts?",
    "answer": "Use the same eligible audience, send volume, billing term and required features in both quotes. Include any ActiveCampaign CRM add-ons and any paid messaging channels. A contact count alone cannot establish which complete setup costs less."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Klaviyo have a free plan, and does ActiveCampaign?",
    "answer": "Klaviyo advertises a free starting option and ActiveCampaign advertises a trial. Confirm current limits and eligibility on their pricing pages. A trial is for proving your workflow; it does not establish the ongoing cost of a production account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which tool is better for wholesale or B2B follow-up on a Shopify store?",
    "answer": "Evaluate ActiveCampaign when you need owned deals and sales-stage automations. Its documentation identifies enhanced CRM add-ons for relevant deal triggers, so include these in the scope and quote. A Shopify wholesale tag and an owned sales opportunity are different records; decide whether you need both."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I move from ActiveCampaign to Klaviyo, or the other way round?",
    "answer": "Plan a controlled workflow rebuild. Preserve permission records, suppressions, event definitions and sales ownership. Disable the old sender for each migrated journey before enabling its replacement, and reconcile queued messages. A CSV import alone does not recreate automations or deal history."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is SMS included in Klaviyo's email plan?",
    "answer": "Do not treat the email quote as an all-channel budget. Check the selected messaging package, destination coverage and allowances on the current pricing page. SMS permission must be handled separately from email permission."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo for Shopify email</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Choose around the work that needs a system of record: order-driven retention, or sales opportunities alongside the store. Klaviyo is a candidate for the first; ActiveCampaign deserves a closer look when wholesale follow-up needs deal ownership. Neither recommendation establishes better revenue, lower cost or faster implementation for your store.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[
          { label: 'Checkout recovery', tool: 'Compare both', reason: 'Both document Shopify recovery. Prove event eligibility and purchase exclusions in your checkout.' },
          { label: 'Wholesale opportunities', tool: 'Evaluate ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Scope the enhanced CRM add-ons needed for deal-triggered work.' },
          { label: 'Order-driven retention', tool: 'Evaluate Klaviyo', reason: 'Its Shopify data reference gives a concrete starting point for checkout and order flows.' },
          { label: 'Existing working system', tool: 'Measure switching cost', reason: 'A new editor does not justify rebuilding functioning workflows by itself.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>The decision for a retail and wholesale store</h2>
        <p>A consumer order and a wholesale opportunity need different treatment. An order records a purchase. An opportunity may still need a sample, a price discussion and a salesperson who owns the next action. If your store only needs order-triggered email, a sales pipeline adds work without solving a stated requirement. If wholesale inquiries are getting lost between a form and a sales inbox, a retention flow alone may leave that problem untouched.</p>
        <p>Write two acceptance criteria before comparing demos: what should stop a recovery message, and who should own an unanswered wholesale inquiry? The first tests purchase state and marketing eligibility. The second tests assignment, deal state and handoff. A platform can meet one criterion and leave the other in a separate system.</p>

        <h2>Shopify event coverage: compare the trigger, not the label</h2>
        <p>ActiveCampaign documents a Shopify integration carrying purchase and abandoned-cart data. Its abandoned-cart documentation includes an automation trigger and a cart content block. That contradicts the idea that Shopify recovery necessarily requires a custom integration. See its <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360012461919-Get-started-with-the-ActiveCampaign-Shopify-integration">Shopify integration overview</a> and <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360001045964-Abandoned-cart-overview">abandoned-cart overview</a>.</p>
        <p>Klaviyo's <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Shopify data reference</a> describes checkout recovery using Checkout Started, a delay and a Placed Order filter. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080407">Shopify setup guide</a> distinguishes synced store data from onsite tracking enabled through the app embed. These are useful implementation details, not proof that every anonymous cart visitor is reachable.</p>
        <div class="card overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>ActiveCampaign evaluation</th><th>Klaviyo evaluation</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Recover an eligible checkout</td><td>Inspect the documented abandoned-cart trigger and content block</td><td>Inspect Checkout Started and the purchase filter</td><td>A permitted test contact enters with the expected products and is excluded after purchase</td></tr>
            <tr><td>React to product interest</td><td>Establish the exact event source and available payload before promising a browse flow</td><td>Check the app embed, identity and Viewed Product tracking on the active theme</td><td>The intended product event appears for an identifiable eligible test contact</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hand a wholesale inquiry to sales</td><td>Check deal-trigger entitlement and assignment in the CRM scope</td><td>Specify the sales system and handoff if the inquiry needs an owned opportunity</td><td>A named owner sees the inquiry and can record the next action</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Use your actual storefront and checkout type for the rehearsal. A button labelled “abandoned cart” is not evidence that both accounts observe the same population. Record what event arrived, the contact identity and the time it became available. Keep a missing event distinct from a correctly suppressed message.</p>

        <h2>CRM scope changes the ActiveCampaign comparison</h2>
        <p>ActiveCampaign's <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/218788707-Automation-triggers-explained">automation trigger reference</a> marks relevant deal triggers as requiring Pipelines or Sales Engagement enhanced CRM add-ons. Do not assume that a base marketing plan includes the sales setup you saw in a demo. Ask for a quote naming the required add-on, users and automations.</p>
        <p>For a proposed wholesale sample journey, separate “requested a sample” from “has an open opportunity.” Decide whether a repeat request should update the same opportunity or create another, who takes over when the assigned salesperson is away, and what closes the loop after an order. A tag is useful for grouping contacts; it does not by itself define ownership or preserve every opportunity for a repeat buyer.</p>
        <p>Reply handling is another acceptance question. Do not assume any reply to any email will stop every sales and marketing journey. Ask the vendor to demonstrate the specific reply trigger available for the message type and account scope you will use. Then test an ordinary reply, an automated response and a reply after reassignment. Record which workflow changes state and which queued sends remain. This is a proposed test, not a claim about observed behavior in either product.</p>

        <h2>A matched cost worksheet</h2>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.activecampaign.com/pricing">ActiveCampaign pricing page</a> and <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo pricing page</a> are the starting points for a current quote. Klaviyo advertises a free start and ActiveCampaign advertises a trial. Neither establishes the production cost of your required setup. This comparison does not reproduce fixed price ladders or claim that every larger account requires a private quote.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Record each vendor's billable audience definition and the number it produces from your own list. Keep unsubscribed and suppressed records available for reconciliation rather than deleting permission history to make the quote smaller.</li>
          <li>Specify campaign sends and automated sends, expected peak usage, paid channels and destinations. Add allowances and overage rules to the quote rather than comparing headline subscriptions.</li>
          <li>For ActiveCampaign, include the CRM scope demonstrated in the wholesale rehearsal. For Klaviyo plus an external CRM, include the second system and any required connector work.</li>
          <li>Separate ongoing subscription and administration from one-time migration work. Record term, currency, taxes, introductory discount expiry and cancellation conditions.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A useful hypothetical budget comparison is the cost of retaining an existing CRM versus moving sales work. If a quoted option saves $40 per month but requires $600 of additional migration work, the simple fee-only payback is $600 ÷ $40 = 15 months. These are illustrative inputs, not vendor prices. The calculation excludes labor after migration and any revenue effect; it is a reason to ask about switching effort, not a reason to predict a return.</p>

        <h2>Proposed rehearsal: one recovery and one sales handoff</h2>
        <p>Use controlled test identities and keep production sends disabled while building. Pick the recovery and sales scenarios that represent your actual business. A consumer-only store can omit the sales handoff and compare the remaining work with its current system.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Create a permitted test checkout and inspect its event and product payload in each account. Repeat with a contact who has declined marketing; confirm the intended eligibility rule rather than equating synced data with permission.</li>
          <li>Complete the purchase while a recovery message is waiting. Inspect the queue and exclusion evaluation before the send point. Then replay or repeat the initiating event to see whether it creates a second recovery path.</li>
          <li>Submit a wholesale sample inquiry for a test buyer who also has a consumer order. Keep the opportunity open while checking that unrelated retail email does not overwrite its owner or sales stage.</li>
          <li>Record a sales reply and close the opportunity. Identify which follow-ups stop, which remain and why. If the workflow crosses a CRM connector, inspect failures and retries on both sides.</li>
          <li>Have the future operator change the recovery delay and reassign the inquiry. Record the steps, permissions required and whether the operator can explain every queued message.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Judge this rehearsal on correct inclusion, exclusions, ownership and maintainability. Time spent is worth recording, but there is no universal launch-time winner. Vendor-attributed orders also do not prove incremental revenue: if that question matters, plan an appropriate controlled holdout separately from this functional test.</p>

        <h2>Migration without losing the sales or permission record</h2>
        <p>Inventory workflows by purpose before exporting contacts. Preserve subscription source and timestamps, channel permissions, suppressions, custom fields and the event definitions that drive branches. Do not discard someone solely because an open-tracking window is quiet; use a documented retention policy and preserve the evidence needed to honor exclusions.</p>
        <p>Map owned opportunities separately from customer tags. Decide which system owns each field and how conflicting updates are resolved. Klaviyo's Shopify setup guide warns that Shopify-synced custom properties can be overwritten on subsequent syncs, so copying values into a new account is not enough to establish long-term ownership.</p>
        <p>Cut over one journey at a time. Disable its previous sender, inspect and reconcile pending messages, then enable the verified replacement. Include Shopify's own recovery and notification settings in the sender inventory. Keep an export and rollback plan until event counts, permission states and queues reconcile. This is a migration procedure to carry out, not a report of completed testing.</p>

        <h2>How to make the choice</h2>
        <p>Shortlist Klaviyo when the unresolved job is Shopify event-driven retention and the sales process already has a home. Shortlist ActiveCampaign when owned wholesale opportunities and marketing automation must work together, provided the quoted CRM scope meets the handoff test. If both pass recovery, the deciding difference may be who can safely operate the sales process and the total cost of maintaining it.</p>
        <p>Keep an existing working setup when neither candidate solves a concrete failure worth the migration effort. Current account entitlements, checkout coverage, reply behavior and all-in costs still require the scoped demonstrations above. No store performance tests or merchant results are claimed here.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign">Drip vs ActiveCampaign</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-mailchimp">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Email apps for supplement stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
